1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,380
Picture the biggest IOU on Earth.

2
00:00:03,380 --> 00:00:05,018
It's signed by the United States

3
00:00:05,018 --> 00:00:06,080
government.

4
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:08,507
It's so long that nobody has ever

5
00:00:08,507 --> 00:00:10,280
read the whole thing.

6
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:12,037
And every single day,

7
00:00:12,037 --> 00:00:13,127
on average,

8
00:00:13,127 --> 00:00:15,352
about six point eight billion dollars more

9
00:00:15,352 --> 00:00:17,509
gets added at the bottom.

10
00:00:17,750 --> 00:00:18,854
On November thirteenth,

11
00:00:18,854 --> 00:00:20,277
the Treasury's own daily count,

12
00:00:20,277 --> 00:00:21,541
called Debt to the Penny,

13
00:00:21,541 --> 00:00:23,560
stood at forty point six trillion dollars.

14
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:26,860
So who actually holds this IOU?

15
00:00:27,110 --> 00:00:29,750
Most people would guess China.

16
00:00:29,970 --> 00:00:32,080
Most people would be wrong.

17
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:32,888
China holds about a penny and a

18
00:00:32,888 --> 00:00:34,040
half of every dollar.

19
00:00:34,140 --> 00:00:37,974
So who holds the rest?

20
00:00:38,217 --> 00:00:40,207
Let's open the vault.

21
00:00:40,207 --> 00:00:41,438
We'll meet the buyers,

22
00:00:41,438 --> 00:00:42,670
the dinner guests,

23
00:00:42,670 --> 00:00:44,906
the warehouse that bought trillions,

24
00:00:44,906 --> 00:00:47,671
and the filing cabinet full of Social

25
00:00:47,671 --> 00:00:49,527
Security's IOUs.

26
00:00:49,557 --> 00:00:51,800
Then we'll check the meter.

27
00:00:52,250 --> 00:00:59,167
If you like finding the hidden money behind the headlines, hit subscribe and like. It really helps the channel.

28
00:01:15,017 --> 00:01:15,680
First,

29
00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:17,667
what is this debt?

30
00:01:17,667 --> 00:01:19,837
It's every Treasury security the

31
00:01:19,837 --> 00:01:21,646
government has issued and not yet paid

32
00:01:21,646 --> 00:01:22,427
back.

33
00:01:22,477 --> 00:01:24,527
It comes in two pieces.

34
00:01:24,657 --> 00:01:27,051
Thirty-two point eight trillion dollars is

35
00:01:27,051 --> 00:01:29,057
debt held by the public.

36
00:01:29,057 --> 00:01:29,528
Bills,

37
00:01:29,528 --> 00:01:32,357
notes and bonds that investors bought.

38
00:01:32,537 --> 00:01:34,529
The other seven point eight trillion

39
00:01:34,529 --> 00:01:36,963
dollars is debt the government owes to

40
00:01:36,963 --> 00:01:37,605
itself,

41
00:01:37,605 --> 00:01:39,311
to its own trust funds.

42
00:01:39,550 --> 00:01:40,930
Let me draw it.

43
00:01:40,930 --> 00:01:43,020
Here's the whole IOU.

44
00:01:43,020 --> 00:01:44,420
Now split it.

45
00:01:44,620 --> 00:01:46,012
The big box is held by the

46
00:01:46,012 --> 00:01:47,010
public.

47
00:01:47,020 --> 00:01:48,900
That means anyone outside the federal

48
00:01:48,900 --> 00:01:49,633
government,

49
00:01:49,633 --> 00:01:52,300
and that includes the Federal Reserve.

50
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:55,470
The smaller box is intragovernmental.

51
00:01:55,750 --> 00:01:57,871
Social Security's trust funds hold about a

52
00:01:57,871 --> 00:01:58,560
third of it,

53
00:01:58,560 --> 00:02:00,220
by our calculation.

54
00:02:00,490 --> 00:02:02,160
Hold on to that box.

55
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:03,757
We'll open it later.

56
00:02:04,017 --> 00:02:05,887
And it keeps growing.

57
00:02:05,887 --> 00:02:07,215
One year earlier,

58
00:02:07,215 --> 00:02:09,383
the total was thirty-eight point two

59
00:02:09,383 --> 00:02:11,067
trillion dollars.

60
00:02:11,157 --> 00:02:13,389
That's about two and a half trillion

61
00:02:13,389 --> 00:02:15,349
dollars added in twelve months,

62
00:02:15,349 --> 00:02:17,829
or roughly six point eight billion dollars

63
00:02:17,829 --> 00:02:18,549
a day,

64
00:02:18,549 --> 00:02:20,637
by our calculation.

65
00:02:20,757 --> 00:02:23,218
The counter passed thirty-eight trillion

66
00:02:23,218 --> 00:02:25,925
in October twenty twenty-five,

67
00:02:25,925 --> 00:02:28,085
thirty-nine trillion in March,

68
00:02:28,085 --> 00:02:31,419
and forty trillion on August eighteenth.

69
00:02:31,684 --> 00:02:34,524
So where does new debt come from?

70
00:02:34,524 --> 00:02:35,684
An auction.

71
00:02:35,684 --> 00:02:37,885
In fiscal year twenty twenty-six,

72
00:02:37,885 --> 00:02:39,887
the Treasury held four hundred and

73
00:02:39,887 --> 00:02:41,199
thirty-six auctions,

74
00:02:41,199 --> 00:02:43,200
and sold nearly thirty-three trillion

75
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:44,957
dollars of securities,

76
00:02:44,957 --> 00:02:46,224
by our count.

77
00:02:46,334 --> 00:02:48,275
Most of that was short-term bills,

78
00:02:48,275 --> 00:02:49,971
paid off and sold again,

79
00:02:49,971 --> 00:02:51,800
over and over.

80
00:02:52,050 --> 00:02:53,730
Take one sale.

81
00:02:53,810 --> 00:02:54,862
On August twelfth,

82
00:02:54,862 --> 00:02:57,440
the Treasury auctioned forty-two billion

83
00:02:57,440 --> 00:02:59,137
dollars of ten-year notes,

84
00:02:59,137 --> 00:03:00,640
and bids came in for more than

85
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:01,670
twice that.

86
00:03:01,900 --> 00:03:03,494
Of what the bidders won,

87
00:03:03,494 --> 00:03:05,804
about three quarters went to indirect

88
00:03:05,804 --> 00:03:06,474
bidders,

89
00:03:06,474 --> 00:03:08,460
who bid through a dealer.

90
00:03:08,470 --> 00:03:11,250
That group includes foreign central banks.

91
00:03:11,300 --> 00:03:14,240
Direct bidders took about fifteen percent.

92
00:03:14,240 --> 00:03:16,958
Big dealer banks took the rest.

93
00:03:17,217 --> 00:03:19,108
And a lot of that paper is

94
00:03:19,108 --> 00:03:20,417
short-dated.

95
00:03:20,567 --> 00:03:22,157
At the end of October,

96
00:03:22,157 --> 00:03:23,143
Treasury bills,

97
00:03:23,143 --> 00:03:24,734
which mature within a year,

98
00:03:24,734 --> 00:03:26,545
made up almost a quarter of the

99
00:03:26,545 --> 00:03:28,739
marketable debt held by the public,

100
00:03:28,739 --> 00:03:30,547
by our calculation.

101
00:03:30,827 --> 00:03:33,877
Short paper has to be refinanced quickly.

102
00:03:34,367 --> 00:03:36,092
Remember that when we get to the

103
00:03:36,092 --> 00:03:37,459
interest bill.

104
00:03:37,717 --> 00:03:40,457
The buyers line up every week.

105
00:03:40,577 --> 00:03:42,928
But who ends up holding the paper?

106
00:03:47,184 --> 00:03:50,086
Picture every foreign holder at one long

107
00:03:50,086 --> 00:03:51,654
dinner table.

108
00:03:51,694 --> 00:03:53,750
In August twenty twenty-six,

109
00:03:53,750 --> 00:03:56,753
according to the Treasury's international

110
00:03:56,753 --> 00:03:58,115
capital data,

111
00:03:58,115 --> 00:04:00,194
foreigners held nine point three trillion

112
00:04:00,194 --> 00:04:03,104
dollars of Treasury securities.

113
00:04:03,394 --> 00:04:05,669
The biggest plate at the table isn't

114
00:04:05,669 --> 00:04:06,774
China's.

115
00:04:06,784 --> 00:04:07,928
It's Japan's,

116
00:04:07,928 --> 00:04:11,694
at one point zero seven trillion dollars.

117
00:04:11,694 --> 00:04:12,434
Second,

118
00:04:12,434 --> 00:04:14,196
the United Kingdom,

119
00:04:14,196 --> 00:04:17,348
at one point zero one trillion dollars.

120
00:04:17,584 --> 00:04:19,084
China comes third,

121
00:04:19,084 --> 00:04:21,626
with five hundred and ninety-one billion

122
00:04:21,626 --> 00:04:22,734
dollars.

123
00:04:22,814 --> 00:04:25,214
And its plate has been shrinking.

124
00:04:25,334 --> 00:04:27,077
Back in twenty thirteen,

125
00:04:27,077 --> 00:04:29,099
China held one point three trillion

126
00:04:29,099 --> 00:04:30,204
dollars.

127
00:04:30,274 --> 00:04:31,103
Today,

128
00:04:31,103 --> 00:04:33,704
it holds less than half of that.

129
00:04:33,950 --> 00:04:37,400
Now compare that with the whole IOU.

130
00:04:37,490 --> 00:04:39,566
China's holdings are about one and a

131
00:04:39,566 --> 00:04:41,414
half percent of the total debt,

132
00:04:41,414 --> 00:04:43,260
by our calculation.

133
00:04:43,490 --> 00:04:44,846
About a penny and a half of

134
00:04:44,846 --> 00:04:46,200
every dollar.

135
00:04:46,370 --> 00:04:48,296
All foreign holders together,

136
00:04:48,296 --> 00:04:50,410
about twenty-three percent.

137
00:04:50,690 --> 00:04:53,489
The idea that China owns America simply

138
00:04:53,489 --> 00:04:55,380
isn't what the data show.

139
00:04:55,617 --> 00:04:57,257
Let me chart it.

140
00:04:57,307 --> 00:04:59,380
China in twenty thirteen,

141
00:04:59,380 --> 00:05:00,747
a tall bar.

142
00:05:00,747 --> 00:05:02,049
China today,

143
00:05:02,049 --> 00:05:03,937
less than half as tall.

144
00:05:03,937 --> 00:05:05,106
Japan today,

145
00:05:05,106 --> 00:05:07,377
the largest foreign holder.

146
00:05:07,537 --> 00:05:08,037
Now,

147
00:05:08,037 --> 00:05:09,064
for scale,

148
00:05:09,064 --> 00:05:10,537
the whole debt.

149
00:05:10,537 --> 00:05:12,795
All three bars fit in one corner

150
00:05:12,795 --> 00:05:13,959
of it.

151
00:05:14,217 --> 00:05:15,737
One caveat.

152
00:05:15,737 --> 00:05:17,677
The Treasury itself warns that these

153
00:05:17,677 --> 00:05:20,047
numbers follow where securities are held

154
00:05:20,047 --> 00:05:21,103
in custody,

155
00:05:21,103 --> 00:05:23,427
not always who really owns them.

156
00:05:23,777 --> 00:05:25,816
Bonds kept in places like Belgium,

157
00:05:25,816 --> 00:05:28,107
Luxembourg or the Cayman Islands may

158
00:05:28,107 --> 00:05:31,227
belong to investors from somewhere else.

159
00:05:31,527 --> 00:05:33,422
So the guest list is a good

160
00:05:33,422 --> 00:05:33,936
map,

161
00:05:33,936 --> 00:05:35,890
not a perfect one.

162
00:05:36,150 --> 00:05:38,448
About three point eight trillion dollars

163
00:05:38,448 --> 00:05:41,256
of the foreign total sits with official

164
00:05:41,256 --> 00:05:41,996
holders,

165
00:05:41,996 --> 00:05:43,502
mostly central banks,

166
00:05:43,502 --> 00:05:46,400
which keep Treasuries as reserves.

167
00:05:46,520 --> 00:05:48,546
When a country needs safe dollars in

168
00:05:48,546 --> 00:05:49,423
a hurry,

169
00:05:49,423 --> 00:05:51,824
this is the pantry it opens.

170
00:05:56,084 --> 00:05:58,060
So if roughly three quarters of the

171
00:05:58,060 --> 00:05:59,135
debt isn't foreign,

172
00:05:59,135 --> 00:06:00,234
who holds it?

173
00:06:00,234 --> 00:06:01,012
Mostly,

174
00:06:01,012 --> 00:06:02,434
Americans.

175
00:06:02,434 --> 00:06:04,854
Here's the Federal Reserve's own

176
00:06:04,854 --> 00:06:05,556
breakdown,

177
00:06:05,556 --> 00:06:08,193
at the end of June twenty twenty-six,

178
00:06:08,193 --> 00:06:10,855
for Treasuries held outside the federal

179
00:06:10,855 --> 00:06:12,181
government.

180
00:06:12,417 --> 00:06:15,124
Money market funds held three point three

181
00:06:15,124 --> 00:06:16,927
trillion dollars.

182
00:06:16,977 --> 00:06:19,827
That's where many people park their cash.

183
00:06:19,927 --> 00:06:21,926
Households held two point eight trillion

184
00:06:21,926 --> 00:06:23,637
dollars directly.

185
00:06:23,817 --> 00:06:26,495
Mutual funds and exchange-traded funds,

186
00:06:26,495 --> 00:06:29,306
about two point six trillion dollars.

187
00:06:29,550 --> 00:06:32,400
Banks and credit unions held about two

188
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:34,140
trillion dollars.

189
00:06:34,140 --> 00:06:35,980
State and local governments,

190
00:06:35,980 --> 00:06:38,150
one point six trillion dollars.

191
00:06:38,150 --> 00:06:39,155
Pension funds,

192
00:06:39,155 --> 00:06:41,410
one point two trillion dollars.

193
00:06:41,410 --> 00:06:42,956
And insurance companies,

194
00:06:42,956 --> 00:06:45,316
about six hundred billion dollars.

195
00:06:45,550 --> 00:06:47,990
So if you have a retirement plan,

196
00:06:47,990 --> 00:06:48,864
a pension,

197
00:06:48,864 --> 00:06:50,879
a money market fund or a bank

198
00:06:50,879 --> 00:06:51,528
account,

199
00:06:51,528 --> 00:06:53,543
there's a decent chance a slice of

200
00:06:53,543 --> 00:06:55,710
this IOU is working for you.

201
00:06:55,860 --> 00:06:57,793
The interest the government pays doesn't

202
00:06:57,793 --> 00:06:58,570
vanish.

203
00:06:58,570 --> 00:07:00,279
A lot of it lands in American

204
00:07:00,279 --> 00:07:01,221
accounts.

205
00:07:01,484 --> 00:07:03,334
Let me draw the loop.

206
00:07:03,334 --> 00:07:05,644
You put savings into a fund.

207
00:07:05,644 --> 00:07:07,684
The fund lends to the Treasury by

208
00:07:07,684 --> 00:07:08,954
buying a bond.

209
00:07:08,954 --> 00:07:11,081
The Treasury pays interest back to the

210
00:07:11,081 --> 00:07:11,814
fund.

211
00:07:11,814 --> 00:07:13,664
And the fund pays you.

212
00:07:13,814 --> 00:07:16,332
One person's debt is another person's

213
00:07:16,332 --> 00:07:17,435
savings.

214
00:07:17,684 --> 00:07:20,238
Then there's the biggest single holder in

215
00:07:20,238 --> 00:07:21,534
the country.

216
00:07:21,584 --> 00:07:23,404
The Federal Reserve.

217
00:07:23,584 --> 00:07:25,536
On November eleventh,

218
00:07:25,536 --> 00:07:27,512
the Fed held four point six trillion

219
00:07:27,512 --> 00:07:30,205
dollars in Treasury securities,

220
00:07:30,205 --> 00:07:32,923
stacked in the warehouse of its balance

221
00:07:32,923 --> 00:07:33,770
sheet.

222
00:07:34,017 --> 00:07:35,935
That's down from a peak of five

223
00:07:35,935 --> 00:07:38,127
point eight trillion dollars in June

224
00:07:38,127 --> 00:07:40,097
twenty twenty-two.

225
00:07:40,367 --> 00:07:41,896
After the pandemic,

226
00:07:41,896 --> 00:07:44,333
the Fed let bonds mature without replacing

227
00:07:44,333 --> 00:07:44,976
them all,

228
00:07:44,976 --> 00:07:47,127
and the shelves thinned out.

229
00:07:47,267 --> 00:07:48,823
But over the past year,

230
00:07:48,823 --> 00:07:50,852
its holdings of short-term bills grew by

231
00:07:50,852 --> 00:07:53,332
about three hundred and eighty-two billion

232
00:07:53,332 --> 00:07:54,383
dollars.

233
00:07:54,617 --> 00:07:55,681
Add it up,

234
00:07:55,681 --> 00:07:56,827
roughly.

235
00:07:56,847 --> 00:07:57,971
Foreign holders,

236
00:07:57,971 --> 00:07:59,717
about a quarter.

237
00:07:59,767 --> 00:08:00,545
The Fed,

238
00:08:00,545 --> 00:08:02,217
about a ninth.

239
00:08:02,327 --> 00:08:03,578
Other investors,

240
00:08:03,578 --> 00:08:05,041
mostly American,

241
00:08:05,041 --> 00:08:06,277
close to half.

242
00:08:06,327 --> 00:08:08,177
And the last big piece?

243
00:08:08,227 --> 00:08:10,886
The one the government owes to itself.

244
00:08:15,150 --> 00:08:17,610
Meet the filing cabinet.

245
00:08:17,760 --> 00:08:18,309
For years,

246
00:08:18,309 --> 00:08:20,583
Social Security collected more than it

247
00:08:20,583 --> 00:08:22,320
paid out in benefits.

248
00:08:22,360 --> 00:08:24,755
The surplus was lent to the Treasury,

249
00:08:24,755 --> 00:08:26,733
in exchange for special Treasury

250
00:08:26,733 --> 00:08:28,931
securities held only inside the

251
00:08:28,931 --> 00:08:30,190
government.

252
00:08:30,350 --> 00:08:31,002
IOUs,

253
00:08:31,002 --> 00:08:33,659
filed in a drawer.

254
00:08:33,917 --> 00:08:36,347
At the end of twenty twenty-five,

255
00:08:36,347 --> 00:08:38,557
those trust fund reserves held two point

256
00:08:38,557 --> 00:08:40,251
five six trillion dollars,

257
00:08:40,251 --> 00:08:42,190
according to the trustees,

258
00:08:42,190 --> 00:08:45,136
as reported by the Congressional Research

259
00:08:45,136 --> 00:08:46,227
Service.

260
00:08:46,437 --> 00:08:48,580
But in twenty twenty-five,

261
00:08:48,580 --> 00:08:49,909
costs beat revenue,

262
00:08:49,909 --> 00:08:52,622
and the reserves shrank by one hundred

263
00:08:52,622 --> 00:08:55,120
and sixty billion dollars.

264
00:08:55,384 --> 00:08:57,784
Let me draw the round trip.

265
00:08:57,904 --> 00:09:00,874
Payroll taxes flow into the trust fund.

266
00:09:00,994 --> 00:09:03,990
Any surplus goes to the Treasury,

267
00:09:03,990 --> 00:09:05,964
which hands back an IOU.

268
00:09:06,174 --> 00:09:08,922
When benefits cost more than taxes bring

269
00:09:08,922 --> 00:09:09,444
in,

270
00:09:09,444 --> 00:09:11,340
the fund hands the IOU back,

271
00:09:11,340 --> 00:09:13,864
and the Treasury pays cash.

272
00:09:14,064 --> 00:09:15,337
To find that cash,

273
00:09:15,337 --> 00:09:17,757
it borrows from the public.

274
00:09:18,017 --> 00:09:20,507
Notice what happens to the total.

275
00:09:20,507 --> 00:09:21,371
Nothing,

276
00:09:21,371 --> 00:09:22,567
at first.

277
00:09:22,737 --> 00:09:25,205
The IOU just moves from the small

278
00:09:25,205 --> 00:09:27,347
box to the big box.

279
00:09:27,557 --> 00:09:30,419
Debt the government owed itself becomes

280
00:09:30,419 --> 00:09:32,689
debt it owes investors.

281
00:09:32,950 --> 00:09:35,560
And the drawer won't last forever.

282
00:09:35,560 --> 00:09:38,004
The trustees project the retirement fund's

283
00:09:38,004 --> 00:09:40,003
reserves run out in late twenty

284
00:09:40,003 --> 00:09:41,270
thirty-two.

285
00:09:41,450 --> 00:09:43,601
Counting the disability fund too,

286
00:09:43,601 --> 00:09:45,968
the combined reserves last into twenty

287
00:09:45,968 --> 00:09:47,160
thirty-four.

288
00:09:47,230 --> 00:09:48,080
After that,

289
00:09:48,080 --> 00:09:50,261
incoming taxes would cover about

290
00:09:50,261 --> 00:09:52,224
eighty-three percent of scheduled

291
00:09:52,224 --> 00:09:53,074
benefits,

292
00:09:53,074 --> 00:09:55,419
unless Congress changes the law.

293
00:09:59,684 --> 00:10:02,244
Which brings us to the meter.

294
00:10:02,294 --> 00:10:04,435
In fiscal year twenty twenty-six,

295
00:10:04,435 --> 00:10:06,143
which ended in September,

296
00:10:06,143 --> 00:10:08,284
the government's net interest bill,

297
00:10:08,284 --> 00:10:10,446
what it pays in interest minus what

298
00:10:10,446 --> 00:10:11,290
it collects,

299
00:10:11,290 --> 00:10:13,236
reached one point zero five trillion

300
00:10:13,236 --> 00:10:13,863
dollars,

301
00:10:13,863 --> 00:10:16,025
according to the Monthly Treasury

302
00:10:16,025 --> 00:10:17,274
Statement.

303
00:10:17,674 --> 00:10:19,352
That's about two point nine billion

304
00:10:19,352 --> 00:10:20,800
dollars every day,

305
00:10:20,800 --> 00:10:22,659
by our calculation.

306
00:10:22,917 --> 00:10:25,248
Now put national defense on the other

307
00:10:25,248 --> 00:10:26,827
side of the scale.

308
00:10:26,827 --> 00:10:27,558
Same year,

309
00:10:27,558 --> 00:10:29,057
same statement.

310
00:10:29,057 --> 00:10:30,939
Nine hundred and sixty-five billion

311
00:10:30,939 --> 00:10:31,887
dollars.

312
00:10:31,887 --> 00:10:33,537
Interest outweighed it.

313
00:10:33,537 --> 00:10:35,137
And it wasn't a one-off.

314
00:10:35,137 --> 00:10:37,034
In fiscal twenty twenty-five,

315
00:10:37,034 --> 00:10:39,380
interest was nine hundred and seventy

316
00:10:39,380 --> 00:10:40,424
billion dollars,

317
00:10:40,424 --> 00:10:41,256
and defense,

318
00:10:41,256 --> 00:10:43,174
nine hundred and seventeen billion

319
00:10:43,174 --> 00:10:44,201
dollars.

320
00:10:44,450 --> 00:10:46,620
Why did the meter speed up?

321
00:10:46,620 --> 00:10:47,740
Rates.

322
00:10:47,850 --> 00:10:49,719
In October twenty twenty-one,

323
00:10:49,719 --> 00:10:51,820
the average interest rate on the

324
00:10:51,820 --> 00:10:54,130
government's marketable debt was one point

325
00:10:54,130 --> 00:10:55,420
four six percent.

326
00:10:55,420 --> 00:10:57,714
By October twenty twenty-six,

327
00:10:57,714 --> 00:11:00,400
it was three point five seven percent.

328
00:11:00,550 --> 00:11:02,509
Cheap old bonds keep maturing,

329
00:11:02,509 --> 00:11:04,989
and they get replaced with pricier new

330
00:11:04,989 --> 00:11:06,085
ones.

331
00:11:06,350 --> 00:11:08,900
Let me put them side by side.

332
00:11:08,900 --> 00:11:09,518
Defense,

333
00:11:09,518 --> 00:11:11,400
fiscal twenty twenty-six.

334
00:11:11,400 --> 00:11:12,358
Net interest,

335
00:11:12,358 --> 00:11:13,720
a bit taller.

336
00:11:13,720 --> 00:11:14,430
Medicare,

337
00:11:14,430 --> 00:11:16,560
about the same height as interest.

338
00:11:16,560 --> 00:11:17,989
Social Security,

339
00:11:17,989 --> 00:11:19,210
taller still.

340
00:11:19,300 --> 00:11:21,459
Interest is now about fourteen cents of

341
00:11:21,459 --> 00:11:23,401
every federal dollar spent,

342
00:11:23,401 --> 00:11:25,179
by our calculation.

343
00:11:25,417 --> 00:11:27,597
And remember who sits on the other

344
00:11:27,597 --> 00:11:29,287
end of that meter.

345
00:11:29,407 --> 00:11:31,477
The holders we met tonight.

346
00:11:31,587 --> 00:11:32,612
Money funds,

347
00:11:32,612 --> 00:11:33,374
pensions,

348
00:11:33,374 --> 00:11:33,873
banks,

349
00:11:33,873 --> 00:11:34,898
households,

350
00:11:34,898 --> 00:11:35,660
the Fed,

351
00:11:35,660 --> 00:11:37,837
and investors abroad.

352
00:11:37,977 --> 00:11:39,745
Every tick of the meter is a

353
00:11:39,745 --> 00:11:41,583
payment to somebody.

354
00:11:41,817 --> 00:11:43,992
And the Congressional Budget Office

355
00:11:43,992 --> 00:11:46,767
expects the meter to keep speeding up.

356
00:11:46,957 --> 00:11:48,971
In its February baseline,

357
00:11:48,971 --> 00:11:51,008
net interest climbs from three point three

358
00:11:51,008 --> 00:11:53,498
percent of the economy this year to

359
00:11:53,498 --> 00:11:55,308
four point six percent by twenty

360
00:11:55,308 --> 00:11:56,587
thirty-six.

361
00:11:56,877 --> 00:11:57,956
The previous high,

362
00:11:57,956 --> 00:11:59,916
going back to nineteen forty,

363
00:11:59,916 --> 00:12:01,435
was three point two percent,

364
00:12:01,435 --> 00:12:03,577
in nineteen ninety-one.

365
00:12:03,717 --> 00:12:05,042
That's a projection,

366
00:12:05,042 --> 00:12:06,540
not a result.

367
00:12:06,784 --> 00:12:10,044
So who actually owns America's debt?

368
00:12:10,044 --> 00:12:10,729
Not China,

369
00:12:10,729 --> 00:12:12,264
at least not much of it.

370
00:12:12,264 --> 00:12:14,674
Foreign investors hold about a quarter,

371
00:12:14,674 --> 00:12:16,894
and Japan holds more than China.

372
00:12:17,064 --> 00:12:19,204
The Fed holds about a ninth.

373
00:12:19,204 --> 00:12:21,449
The government owes almost a fifth to

374
00:12:21,449 --> 00:12:22,041
itself,

375
00:12:22,041 --> 00:12:24,274
mostly to future retirees.

376
00:12:24,324 --> 00:12:26,212
And most of the rest belongs to

377
00:12:26,212 --> 00:12:27,368
Americans,

378
00:12:27,368 --> 00:12:28,524
through money funds,

379
00:12:28,524 --> 00:12:28,972
banks,

380
00:12:28,972 --> 00:12:31,224
pensions and their own savings.

381
00:12:31,334 --> 00:12:33,500
The biggest lender to the United States

382
00:12:33,500 --> 00:12:35,354
is the United States.

383
00:12:35,354 --> 00:12:39,266
So every dollar of interest is two

384
00:12:39,266 --> 00:12:40,634
things at once.

385
00:12:40,634 --> 00:12:41,737
A cost for taxpayers,

386
00:12:41,737 --> 00:12:44,018
and income for whoever holds the IOU.

387
00:12:44,284 --> 00:12:44,856
Next time,

388
00:12:44,856 --> 00:12:47,417
we follow the money behind another hidden

389
00:12:47,417 --> 00:12:49,594
link of the world economy.

390
00:12:49,704 --> 00:12:51,918
This is The Hidden Economics.
